WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>I have a young man who just turned 18. He

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.720
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>wants to move in with a friend and work at

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.080
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>a grocery store. His parents want guardianship. Honestly, I worry

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.104
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>about him signing a lease he does not understand.

00:00:13.454 --> 00:00:16.587
<v Human Services Supervisor>That is a real worry. Before anyone talks about guardianship,

00:00:16.587 --> 00:00:19.094
<v Human Services Supervisor>what has he said he wants help with?

00:00:19.444 --> 00:00:22.884
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>I have mostly talked with his mother. She calls, and

00:00:22.884 --> 00:00:24.604
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>he is usually at school.

00:00:24.954 --> 00:00:28.215
<v Human Services Supervisor>That happens a lot. The parent is the one on

00:00:28.215 --> 00:00:31.476
<v Human Services Supervisor>the phone. But under Minnesota law, a court can appoint

00:00:31.476 --> 00:00:34.738
<v Human Services Supervisor>a guardian only if his needs cannot be met in

00:00:34.738 --> 00:00:37.999
<v Human Services Supervisor>a less restrictive way. Supported decision making is one of

00:00:37.999 --> 00:00:41.260
<v Human Services Supervisor>the alternatives named. So his own view is the starting

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:41.586
<v Human Services Supervisor>point.

00:00:41.936 --> 00:00:45.866
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>Does supported decision making actually work? I hear a lot

00:00:45.866 --> 00:00:48.224
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>of enthusiasm and not many numbers.

00:00:48.574 --> 00:00:52.310
<v Human Services Supervisor>That is a fair challenge. Reviews call the evidence limited.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:56.046
<v Human Services Supervisor>Pilots are small. A 2025 DHS report says early results

00:00:56.046 --> 00:00:59.782
<v Human Services Supervisor>suggest better well-being, but it is still being studied. What

00:00:59.782 --> 00:01:03.517
<v Human Services Supervisor>is known is that young people with stronger self-determination are

00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:07.253
<v Human Services Supervisor>more likely to have jobs and community access a year

00:01:07.253 --> 00:01:08.374
<v Human Services Supervisor>after high school.

00:01:08.724 --> 00:01:11.673
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>So the case for choice is strong even if the

00:01:11.673 --> 00:01:12.852
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>program evidence is thin.

00:01:13.202 --> 00:01:16.901
<v Human Services Supervisor>Yes. And the law is clear. Now, your worry about

00:01:16.901 --> 00:01:19.490
<v Human Services Supervisor>the lease. What exactly could go wrong?

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.101
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>He might agree to rent he cannot afford, or a

00:01:23.101 --> 00:01:25.384
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>lease with terms he does not follow.

00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:29.116
<v Human Services Supervisor>Then ask him who he trusts to look at contracts

00:01:29.116 --> 00:01:32.498
<v Human Services Supervisor>with him before he signs. An uncle, a friend, a

00:01:32.498 --> 00:01:35.879
<v Human Services Supervisor>benefits counselor. That addresses the specific risk and leaves the

00:01:35.879 --> 00:01:36.894
<v Human Services Supervisor>decision with him.

00:01:37.244 --> 00:01:39.860
<v Lead Agency Case Manager>His mother may still want guardianship.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:43.510
<v Human Services Supervisor>She may. Listen to what she fears most. Often a

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:46.810
<v Human Services Supervisor>fear about money or safety has a specific answer. If

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.110
<v Human Services Supervisor>she still goes to court, the court has to consider

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.410
<v Human Services Supervisor>alternatives and explain why they would not work. What he

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:56.050
<v Human Services Supervisor>wants, and which supports were tried, will matter.
